# PowerShell shell (Windows)

The agent shell on this setup is PowerShell. Do not use bash-only constructs in it.

## Forbidden in PowerShell

- Heredoc: `$(cat <<'EOF' ... EOF)`
- Unquoted git upstream: `@{u}` (PowerShell parses `@{...}` as a hashtable). Write `'@{u}'`.
- `export FOO=bar`, bash arrays, `&&` chains that assume bash semantics for `$?`.

## Use instead

- Multi-line text for a commit: a here-string.

```powershell
git commit -m @"
type(scope): subject

body
"@
```

  `git commit -F -` with a here-string does not work: PowerShell passes the text as an argument and git reads it as a pathspec (S8b). Use `-m` as above, or commit from Bash with a heredoc, or write the message to a scratch file and pass `-F <file>`. V3a used `-F -` again; nothing was committed, and the push that followed made the branch on GitHub at main's commit (PR #369).
- The output of a native command (`gh pr view --json body --jq .body`) comes back as an array of lines. `[IO.File]::WriteAllText($file, $lines)` joins them with spaces: in V3a a PR body edited that way became one line, and was restored from its edit history. Join first: `(gh ...) -join "`n"` (PR #369).
- In an array of pairs, the comma binds before `+`: `` @('old', 'new' + "`n" + 'more') `` is three elements, and a loop over the pairs replaces with `'new'` alone. I1's design lost the end of a code block and three paragraphs this way, while each replacement counted one match (PR #385). Make a multi-line replacement with the editor tool, one place at a time; after any scripted replacement, read the changed lines in the file, not only the count of matches.
- The repository's text files have LF line ends. A replacement whose old text holds `` "`r`n" `` matches nothing and says nothing; count the matches before the replacement is trusted (PR #369).
- Long text for `gh`: write a temporary UTF-8 file and pass `--body-file`, then delete it (`.cursor/rules/scratch.mdc`).
- Do not put `Remove-Item` in the same command as `$env:PATH += ';C:\Program Files ...'`: the agent's PowerShell tool reads `C:\Program` as the item to remove and refuses the whole command (twice in S8a). Remove files in a separate call, or from Bash.
- A helper script (Python, a mutant patch): write it to a file in the scratchpad first, then run it. Do not build it from a here-string piped to `Set-Content` in the same command that removes files: in S5 the agent's PowerShell tool read a line break of the here-string as a path for `Remove-Item` and refused the whole command.
- An array that holds one array, `@( @('a', 'b', 'c') )`, is flattened by the outer `@()`: a loop over it gets the strings, not the one row. In I1a a mutant script's list of one mutant ran with an empty path. Write one row as `,@('a', 'b', 'c')` (PR #389).
- A refused command runs none of its parts, the ones before the refused part included. In I1a `gh pr edit` and a removal of its body file went in one command; the whole was refused, and the PR body was not changed. Remove files in a command of their own, and after a refusal check whether the earlier parts ran (PR #389).
- Native commands set `$LASTEXITCODE`. Check it before the next step: `dotnet test ...; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { exit $LASTEXITCODE }`.
- Variable names are not case-sensitive. A local `$low` is the parameter `$Low`: in L8 a loop that set `$low` overwrote the run count, and the measurement did not end. Give locals names that differ from every parameter in more than case (`$fewer`, not `$low`).
- Do not name a variable `$input`, `$args`, `$_`, or another automatic variable: PowerShell sets them itself. `$input` is the pipeline's input; in I1b a mutant script that kept a mutant's text in `$input` read something else (PR #397).
- Splatting one string (`& $app @args` where `$args` is `'--text'`) passes its characters one by one. Build arguments as an array: `[string[]]$arguments = @(...)`. In T4 five captures at 100 % ran without their scene's arguments before this was found (PR #173).
- Run `./build.ps1` in PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, the agent's PowerShell tool), not Windows PowerShell 5.1 (`powershell.exe`): 5.1 has no `$IsWindows`, and `vendor/build.ps1` stops (T2).
- A scratch build that reads an environment variable is run from Bash, with `VAR=value cmd` and `env -u VAR cmd`. In T8, after `[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(name, $null)` in PowerShell, the child processes still saw the variable, and all three captures, one meant to have it unset, came out the same (the cause was not traced; PR #198's retrospective).
- Quote paths with spaces. Prefer repository-relative paths, except for .NET methods: `[IO.File]::ReadAllText` and the like read a relative path from the process's working directory, not from `Set-Location`. Pass them an absolute path. In T7 a replacement in a worktree reported its text missing (PR #196).
- In Bash, `rm -rf "$DIR"/*` is refused by the agent's safety check, because an empty `$DIR` would make it `/*`. Write `"${DIR:?}"/*` or a literal path (S8b).
- A P/Invoke declared with `Add-Type` receives `$null` for a string parameter as an empty string. Pass `[NullString]::Value` for a null string: `FindWindowW("TsfSpike", $null)` returned 0, and the messages meant for the window went nowhere (PR #511).
- In PowerShell, a command that holds `C:\Program Files (x86)\...` (the `vswhere` path a NativeAOT publish adds to `PATH`) and calls `Remove-Item` is refused by the same check as a removal of a system path, and none of it runs. Clean up in a command of its own (T5, PR #228). I1c broke this twice: a removal beside the `vswhere` path, and one beside a search whose pattern held `\*\*`, which the check read as a path; both commands ran nothing (PR #410). Give `Remove-Item` a command of its own every time, whatever else the command holds.

## Writing files

A body posted with `gh` that holds a value from a command is written to a file first, with the value already in it, or given through an unquoted heredoc: a quoted heredoc (`<<'EOF'`) keeps `$(...)` as text. A reply on PR #496 was posted with `head $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)` in its title and was corrected after.

Write files with the editor tools, not with shell redirection. A Python script sent through the Bash tool loses the backslashes of `\b`, `\f`, `\r`, `\t`, `\v`, and `\n` in its string literals (PR #492's placement check: a regular expression's `\b` became a backspace byte, and the script compared nothing; T13: a list of mutants given through a heredoc had its `\n` turned into line breaks, and did not run; PR #438): in T4 an edit of `XylemUI.Text.targets` did not match `static\freetype.lib`, and one of `build.ps1` wrote a carriage return into `gpui-text-target\release` (PR #173). Edit text that holds backslashes with the editor tool. A script that another script writes is written with the editor tool too: nested quoting fails in ways that only show after a long run. In R4b a Python edit wrote the C# escape of a UTF-8 literal's terminator as the byte 0 itself into `QuadShaders.cs`; the build passed, `grep` then took the file for a binary, and the review found it (PR #256). After an edit by a script, count the control bytes in the files it wrote with `tr -cd '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037' < <file> | wc -c`; the count is 0. `grep -cP '[\x00-\x08\x0B\x0C\x0E-\x1F]'` counts no byte 0: in R4c a Python edit wrote two of them into `D3D11Renderer.cs`, and the check this line gave before found neither (PR #264's retrospective).

The Edit tool does not match a line that holds a `\uXXXX` escape in a C# string (`TextWrapTests`' `nn\u00ADnn`), in either form, and a Python script sent through Bash loses the backslash of `\\u` and does not run. Edit the ranges before and after the line, and write a script that holds such text to a file first (PR #325, and again when this rule was written).

`Copy-Item` keeps the file's last write time, so an incremental build does not see a file put back from a copy and keeps the old output. In R4c a shader variant copied over the source was not built, and the other variant was measured. After copying a source file into place, set its time to now (`(Get-Item <file>).LastWriteTime = Get-Date`) (PR #264). I1a broke this rule again: main's `WindowInput.cs` copied back over a variant was not built, and the figures of the two builds that PR #389 gave were measured again with the executables' hashes checked (#390's PR).
